  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Goal**\n\nI\'m focusing now on the user\'s explicit request. My primary task is adding an image subtly behind \"THOMAS NGUYEN\" on the landing page. It needs to be slightly blurred, a faded background visual effect.\n\n\n**Implementing the Enhancement**\n\nI\'ve examined the current hero section code within `App.tsx` and am now formulating how to add the background image. The plan is to insert an image element into the hero section. My goal is to add the image element inside the section. The image will be positioned behind the text, with adjusted opacity and blur to achieve the desired subtle background effect.\n\n\n**Refining the Approach**\n\nI\'m now evaluating image integration strategies. An `img` tag with absolute positioning behind the \"THOMAS NGUYEN\" text seems optimal, using `absolute inset-0 object-cover opacity-10` or a similar configuration, potentially with `mix-blend-luminosity` and `blur-sm`. I will place the image within the Hero `<section>` to keep it as an absolute background element. I\'ve selected a suitable placeholder image from Unsplash, specifically a shadowy professional figure, which the user can later replace, using `https://images.unsplash.com/photo-155074516` as a starting point.\n\n\n**Selecting the Image**\n\nI\'ve decided on a placeholder image from Unsplash: `https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507003211169-0a1dd7228f2d?q=80&w=1000&auto=format&fit=crop`, a portrait that\'s suitable for initial integration. To achieve the \"m\341\273\235 m\341\273\235\" (subtle/faded) effect, I will leverage `opacity-20`, `blur-[2px]`, `mix-blend-luminosity`, and a radial mask for smooth blending. I\'m now structuring the background image\'s CSS within the hero section.\n\n\n**Refining the Position**\n\nI\'m now implementing the placement of the background image. After considering several approaches, I believe placing a div absolutely behind the h1 will produce the best visual effect. I\'m focusing on CSS classes like `inset-0`, `z-0`, `flex`, `items-center`, `justify-center`, and `pointer-events-none` to control the image position and prevent interaction. The image source is set to `https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1576825946864-cf3d3b7fa166?q=80&w=1920&auto=format&fit=crop`, using the provided filter and blend modes for desired effect.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now revising the mask gradient approach. Previously, I was using an inline style for the `mask-image`. I\'ve realized that using CSS `mask-image` is better for a true fade-out so the background gradient shows through. The current approach uses a radial gradient with black at 0% and transparent at 70%.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Integration**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `maskImage` as a radial gradient to ensure a smooth transition, which is looking very elegant and will achieve the intended subtle fade out of the background image. I\'ve now opened `App.tsx` and located the hero section, ready to make the final modifications, as a new hero section has been written.\n\n\n**Adjusting the Techniques**\n\nI\'m now implementing the code, and realized `filter: blur()` combined with `mask-image` causes rendering issues in Safari. I\'m choosing to instead blur the image itself via class, for cross-browser compatibility, and have updated the CSS. The approach is solid, so I\'m moving forward with this revised method.\n\n\n**Confirming the Opacity**\n\nI am now fine-tuning the opacity, aiming for a subtle effect: either 0.15 or 0.2. Blending with mix-blend-luminosity looks good. I\'m verifying the text container and making it `relative z-10`, to ensure the text is well in front of the new background.\n\n\n**Implementing the Background**\n\nI\'ve completed the code implementation with a `motion.div` for a subtle fade-in, and it\'s positioned absolutely. I\'m using a radial gradient `maskImage` for a smooth fade-out and setting the image\'s opacity to `0.15` for a very subtle effect. I also set `pointer-events-none` to improve usability.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
