    thinking: "**Reviewing the Image Issue**\n\nOkay, I\'ve started by focusing on the image request. The user is still seeing an invisible background, even after trying the URL again and uploading the actual sunset image. The image is horizontal, depicting a person\'s profile by the sea. I\'m carefully reviewing these details to pinpoint the source of the persistent invisibility. I\'m trying to identify where things could be failing.\n\n\n**Analyzing Code & Policies**\n\nOkay, I\'m now digging into the code, especially the section handling the hero background. The image is loaded within a `motion.div` element. I\'m focusing on CSS properties like `maskImage`, `WebkitMaskImage`, `filter`, and `opacity`, to see if they\'re the cause. I will also check the iframe setup and any Content Security Policy (CSP) configurations to rule out external interference.\n\n\n**Investigating Visibility Concerns**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the z-index hierarchy and reviewing the relationship of the `main` container, `hero` content, `.noise-overlay`, and `.glow-effect` to identify potential layering issues. The `.glow-effect` is outside of `<main>`, so it is unlikely to be the problem, but I am still not completely sure about the exact relationship and how that applies to the `<img` element. I\'m also suspecting a URL issue: perhaps Cloudfront is blocking the image requests due to a missing or incorrect referrer policy, or some signature parameter or expiration issue. It seems weird to be a CORS issue, if it\'s just an image rendered, but I\'m open to the possibility that some COEP/COOP policy might block the image.\n\n\n**Refining Display Properties**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on display properties. The `mask-image` property is a likely culprit for clipping. I\'ll test compatibility. I\'m also examining size and layout; the parent should have `relative` for centering to work. The React/Motion `opacity` could also be failing to animate smoothly. I\'ll simplify, increasing opacity to `0.6` to test, and use a simpler `maskImage`. I\'ll also add `referrerPolicy=\"no-referrer\"` to the `src`.\n\n\n**Simplifying for Visibility**\n\nI\'m now changing my focus to ensuring the image is visible first and foremost. Removing the mask is the first step, switching instead to opacity and a gradient overlay for blending. I\'ll make the background component absolute, ensuring it\'s inset-0. Inside, the image will fill the space with `object-cover` and reduced opacity. Then, I\'ll layer fading gradients from top and bottom to blend with the background, using both a `to-t` and `to-b` gradient to ensure visibility. I\'m revisiting the `App.tsx` file for precision edits.\n\n\n**Adjusting Overlay Blending**\n\nOkay, I\'m now modifying the blending to ensure compatibility with the `body`\'s linear gradient background. I realized the radial gradient might create a hard circle if the underlying background isn\'t a solid color. Instead, I\'ll allow the linear background to show through by reducing image opacity.\n\n\n**Simplifying URL & Structure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the provided image URL and simplifying the background structure. I\'m removing the `mask-image` for a clearer display, testing a more basic `opacity` to verify visibility, and double-checking the URL validity. Finally, I\'m replacing the background `motion.div` with a more basic structure.\n\n\n**Refining Opacity & Masks**\n\nI\'m currently focusing on the `motion.div` element\'s opacity and mask properties. I\'m exploring different opacity values and mask gradients to achieve the desired blend. The code is being refined to ensure the image appears with the intended visual effect. I\'m prioritizing direct visibility and testing different parameters. The goal is to optimize both aesthetic appeal and performance.\n\n\n**Refining Background Image**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the background image with a specific URL: the goal is better visibility. The existing code uses `blur-[2px]`, but I\'m checking the opacity and ensuring the `motion.img` element works, and verifying that the CSS `filter` blur is appropriate for the effect. My tests will focus on the interplay of both `opacity` and CSS `filter`. I also need to confirm the z-index hierarchy is correct.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Image Clarity**\n\nNow, I\'m verifying the image clarity with the specific URL provided, focusing on ensuring the correct display and opacity. I\'ve re-examined the blurring, and I\'m ensuring the z-index hierarchy is correct. I am focused on layering and the gradients. The current gradient setup appears to be correct.\n\n\n**Optimizing Image Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on optimizing the image display using `object-cover` with an opacity of `0.5`, to balance visibility and the gradient. I\'m swapping the `from-[#050505]` color for a more standard `from-black` to match the existing design, and avoid Tailwind\'s opacity modifier complications. I\'m focusing on the code section with the background image to ensure the photo renders as expected. I\'m verifying the `object-cover` and opacity combination will result in the desired aesthetic.\n\n\n**Refining Gradient Overlay**\n\nI\'m now refining the gradient overlay for the image. I\'ve re-examined the code and am adjusting the gradient\'s alpha values, to better blend it with the underlying background and ensure the image stands out clearly. My goal is a subtle, yet effective, integration. I\'m focusing on the interplay between the `opacity` and gradient colors.\n\n\n**Simplifying the Background Display**\n\nI\'m now simplifying the background display for clarity, removing `blur-[2px]` and focusing on a standard opacity level, starting with `0.5`, to ensure the image is visible. The goal is to verify the image renders correctly before re-introducing any effects. I\'m focusing on the layering approach, the absolute positioning, and the use of `object-cover`. I will make sure the edges are not too harsh, fading to transparent with opacity, and avoid any color clashes.\n\n\n"
